arm64: dts: h713: the focus motor's range gate needs the pull-up

The limiter on PH14 is a switch to ground that closes outside the allowed travel and is open
inside it. Stock's driver sets no bias and neither does ours, and on our boot chain the pad
came up with none: a floating input, which reads a stale level. On 23.09.2026 the HY310 came
up reading "in range" while the focus stood 110 msteps above its travel, no edge was ever
latched on the way there, and h713-focus and h713-autofocus refused every move.

Measured through the PIO registers the same day, at the running device: with pull-down the
pad reads LOW at every position (the switch cannot drive it HIGH), with pull-up it reads HIGH
over the travel, drops to LOW at the edge within 5 msteps and comes back 10 msteps in. So the
pull-up goes into a pinctrl group on the motor node, applied before the driver probes. The
stock pinconf capture that says "pull down" for PH14 cannot be the level the vendor's driver
works with, or it would read "outside" everywhere; the comment in the tree said the driver
sets the bias itself, which it has not done since 0153.
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -638,6 +638,18 @@
 				function = "gpio_in";
 			};
 
+			/*
+			 * The focus motor's range gate. The switch pulls PH14 to
+			 * ground outside the allowed travel and leaves it open
+			 * inside, so the pad needs the pull-up to read HIGH there:
+			 * measured on the HY310 on 23.09.2026 (see the motor node).
+			 */
+			motor_limiter_pin: motor-limiter-pin {
+				pins = "PH14";
+				function = "gpio_in";
+				bias-pull-up;
+			};
+
 
 
 			/*
@@ -2465,17 +2477,31 @@
 	 *
 	 * PH14 is a range gate, not an end stop: it reads active over
 	 * the whole allowed travel, and the driver reverses when it
-	 * goes inactive (motor_run_up_control 0xc05d6ea0).  On stock it
-	 * is a GPIO input with a pull-down, which is the idle level for
-	 * active_level = 1 (re/captures/HY310-DEV/stock_pinconf_pio.txt,
-	 * "pin 238 (PH14): input bias pull down").  The driver sets that
-	 * bias itself, so no pinctrl group is needed here.
+	 * goes inactive (motor_run_up_control 0xc05d6ea0).  The switch
+	 * is a contact to ground that closes outside the travel; inside
+	 * it is open, and the level there is whatever the pad is biased
+	 * to.  Stock's driver sets no bias, and neither does ours.  On
+	 * our boot chain the pad came up with none at all, and a floating
+	 * input reads a stale level: on 23.09.2026 it read "in range" at
+	 * boot while the mechanism stood 110 msteps above its travel,
+	 * and the guarded moves never latched an edge, so both tools
+	 * refused to move it.  Measured the same day through the PIO
+	 * registers: with pull-down the pad reads LOW at every position
+	 * (the switch cannot drive it HIGH), with pull-up it reads HIGH
+	 * over the travel, drops to LOW at the edge within 5 msteps and
+	 * returns 10 msteps back in.  So the pull-up is the pinctrl
+	 * group's, applied before the driver probes.  The stock capture
+	 * ("pin 238 (PH14): input bias pull down", re/captures/HY310-DEV/
+	 * stock_pinconf_pio.txt) cannot be the level the vendor's driver
+	 * works with, or it would read "outside" everywhere.
 	 *
 	 * GPIO format: <&pio bank pin flags>  bank 7 = PH
 	 */
 	motor_ctr: motor-ctr {
 		compatible = "allwinner,hy310-focus-motor", "motor-control";
 		status = "okay";
+		pinctrl-names = "default";
+		pinctrl-0 = <&motor_limiter_pin>;
 
 		motor-phase-num = <4>;
 		motor-gpio-phase0 = <&pio 7 4 0>;	/* PH4 */
